---
import ServicePageLayout from '../../layouts/ServicePageLayout.astro';

const data = {
  metaTitle: "Software Project Rescue & App Takeover Australia | AWLabs",
  metaDescription: "Developer gone or app half finished? We take over stalled and failed software projects in Australia: secure your accounts, audit the code, then fix or finish.",
  category: "Project Rescue",
  serviceName: "Software Project Rescue",
  nounPhrase: "a software project rescue",
  involveHeading: "What happens when we take over a stalled or failed project?",
  whyHeading: "How do we rescue software we didn't write?",
  techHeading: "Which stacks can we take over?",
  processHeading: "How does a project takeover run, step by step?",
  whoHeading: "Which businesses ask us to take over their software?",
  fitHeading: "Is a rescue the right move for your project?",
  faqHeading: "Questions people ask when a developer has left",
  slug: "software-project-rescue",
  heroHeadline: "Developer Gone, App Half Finished? Find Out What Is Worth Saving",
  heroTagline: "We take over software projects that have stalled, failed or been abandoned: we help you secure your accounts, tell you plainly what state the code is in, then fix it, finish it or rebuild only the parts that need it.",
  summaryStatement: "A software project rescue is the takeover of an app or system that another developer or agency left unfinished, unstable or unsupported: the new team secures the code, hosting and accounts, assesses what exists, and then stabilises, finishes or partly rebuilds it under a fixed scope, rather than starting again by default.",
  description: [
    "Projects stall for ordinary reasons. A freelancer takes a full time job, an agency closes or loses the developer who knew the system, the budget runs out at 80% done, or the app works in a demo but falls over with real users. Whatever the cause, the owner is left with the same questions: what do we actually have, can anyone else work on it, and how much more will it cost to get it working? A rescue answers those questions in order, and the first answer can be better news than people expect. Code that feels like a write off is sometimes sound underneath, with the real problems concentrated in a few places: deployment, integrations, missing tests or one badly built feature.",
    "The first job is not code at all. It is making sure you control the things your software depends on. In the first 48 hours we help you check who holds each of these, and in whose name: the code repository; the hosting or cloud account; the domain registrar and DNS; the Apple Developer and Google Play developer accounts; the production database and its backups; API keys and the billing accounts for third party services such as payments, email, SMS and maps; and the design files. Anything held only in a departed developer's personal account is a risk to fix before any development starts, because a lapsed card or a closed account can take a live app offline.",
    "Then we assess the codebase the way we would assess any system we did not write: senior engineers read the critical paths, automated scans check dependencies and committed secrets, and we try to build and deploy it from scratch, which is the quickest test of whether a project can be handed over at all. The result is a written assessment with three things you can act on: what works and can be kept, what is broken or risky and what it would cost to fix, and a recommendation to fix, finish or rebuild for each part of the system. Sometimes the honest recommendation is a rebuild. More often it is a short stabilisation phase followed by finishing the features that were never completed.",
    "Getting code and accounts back from a previous developer depends on what was agreed and on that developer's cooperation, so we never promise it. What we can do is tell you exactly what is missing, prepare a precise list of what to request, and work from whatever you do own. Under the Copyright Act 1968, copyright in work made by an employee in the course of employment generally belongs to the employer, while copyright in commissioned work usually stays with the author unless it has been assigned, which is why the contract with your previous developer matters. This is general information, not legal advice; for a dispute, speak to a lawyer, and we can supply the technical facts they need.",
    "One of our video testimonials is a rescue. Dave runs a construction company and had paid another provider to build a platform for his bookings, invoicing and client follow ups. In his words, \"That platform was a mess.\" We dug into the existing platform rather than starting again, and fixed his invoicing and lead chasing. Dave put it this way: \"I'd written the whole thing off in my head and Andy and the team brought it back to life for me.\" His advice to anyone in the same spot: \"There might be a lot more worth saving than what you think.\" His full story is on our client stories page.",
  ],
  benefits: [
    {
      icon: '<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="3" y="11" width="18" height="11" rx="2"/><path d="M7 11V7a5 5 0 0 1 10 0v4"/></svg>',
      title: "Your Accounts Secured First",
      description: "Before we change a line of code, we map every account your software depends on and who holds it, then help you move each one into your organisation's name with us added as a user. That protects the live app from a lapsed subscription or a former developer closing their account.",
    },
    {
      icon: '<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8"/><path d="m21 21-4.3-4.3"/></svg>',
      title: "A Plain Account of the Code",
      description: "You get a written assessment a non technical owner can follow and an engineer can verify: what works, what is broken, what is risky, and what each fix would cost. Findings link to the files and scan results behind them, so nothing rests on our opinion alone.",
    },
    {
      icon: '<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M16 3h5v5"/><path d="M8 3H3v5"/><path d="M12 22v-8.3a4 4 0 0 0-1.17-2.83L3 3"/><path d="m15 9 6-6"/></svg>',
      title: "Fix, Finish or Rebuild, Part by Part",
      description: "We decide module by module, not all or nothing. Sound parts are kept, fragile parts are stabilised, and only the parts that cost more to repair than to replace are rebuilt. A full rebuild is recommended when the foundations, such as the data model or security, cannot carry the product.",
    },
    {
      icon: '<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 3l1.9 5.8H20l-4.9 3.6 1.9 5.8L12 14.6l-5 3.6 1.9-5.8L4 8.8h6.1z"/></svg>',
      title: "Apps Built With AI Tools Welcome",
      description: "Many stalled projects today began in an AI app builder or a no code platform and hit a wall at logins, payments, data or scale. We assess them the same way: keep what is sound, fix insecure defaults and committed secrets, and move the parts that outgrew the tool onto a maintainable stack.",
    },
    {
      icon: '<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M14 2H6a2 2 0 0 0-2 2v16a2 2 0 0 0 2 2h12a2 2 0 0 0 2-2V8z"/><polyline points="14 2 14 8 20 8"/><line x1="8" y1="13" x2="16" y2="13"/><line x1="8" y1="17" x2="13" y2="17"/></svg>',
      title: "A Handover Pack You Keep",
      description: "Every takeover produces documents that stay with you whoever works on the software next: an inventory of the system and its services, an access map showing who holds what, a risk register, and a run book explaining how to build, deploy, back up and restore it.",
    },
    {
      icon: '<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M21 15a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H7l-4 4V5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h14a2 2 0 0 1 2 2z"/></svg>',
      title: "No Blame, Straight Answers",
      description: "Rescue work goes faster without a post mortem on the last team. We describe the code as it is, tell you what we would do and why, and say so when the cheaper option is the right one, including when that means keeping more of the existing work than you expected.",
    },
  ],
  useCases: [],
